951 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 649 0 R
/E (IMPLICATIONS AND PRIORITIES)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
952 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 649 0 R
/E (The report identifies six priorities for schools, governments, regulators, AI developers and EdTech providers, and funders including philanthropies, multilateral and industry actors who fund education. All six face the same constraint: time. AI tools can reach classrooms before evidence on their effects is available, or before teachers are prepared to use them. The benefits of education investment unfold over years or decades, while political mandates and funding commitments are often much shorter. This mismatch tends to leave long-term investments underfunded, such as teacher training, evaluation, and programme continuity  \(Chapter IV\). The priorities below therefore favour commitments that can outlast political cycles: multi-year funding, recurrent budget lines and investment in human capacity. They complement public investment in regulators able to audit AI products used by children. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
953 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 649 0 R
/E (The following priorities are organized around the same logic as the key findings: ffrom the people around each child to the systems that support them, ending with our recommendation of a Universal Learning & AI Literacy Toolkit:)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
954 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 649 0 R
/E (Investing in teachers, their training, and in dedicated time for digital and AI literacy. Teachers should help design curricula as it helps reflect classroom needs and build confidence in programme design. Training should begin in initial teacher education and continue throughout their careers, within paid working time. All teachers need training relevant to their subjects, with deeper preparation for those teaching digital and AI literacy. They also need safe, open or sovereign AI tools for planning lessons, administration and supporting students with special educational needs. Human oversight and relational mentoring in AI-mediated learning, as well as preserved shared references and collective discussion across students should be required by funders. Training and tools reach students only if digital and AI literacy has a defined place in the schedule: systems must decide whether it becomes a subject, is integrated into existing ones or become cross-curricular projects, and what it replaces \(Section 2.1.1\). This decision also shapes curriculum development, evaluation and alignment with AI competency frameworks.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
