1067 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 655 0 R
/E ( principals reported any AI policy \(Doss et al., 2025\). Three conditions are currently driving this: \(i\) evidence on how AI affects learning is still emerging, and the first large-scale data, such as PISA 2025, are observational, \(ii\) AI capabilities and uses change faster than curricula, teacher training and regulation can adapt, and \(iii\) the public debate is largely framed around access to tools rather than around what schools are for and what children need to learn. The deploy-or-restrict framing therefore leaves two challenges unresolved: determining which AI uses and designs are educationally appropriate at different ages, and preparing children to navigate general-purpose AI tools they encounter regardless of school policies. The first requires evidence of educational impact and appropriate pedagogical guidance. The second calls for digital and AI literacy, alongside product safeguards and regulation. Both require teacher preparation and coordination between school and home.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1068 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 655 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1069 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 655 0 R
/E (Schools pursue these purposes, but many of the decisions that shape them are taken at the government level. DL and AI literacy sit at the crossroads of several policy agendas: curriculum and pedagogy \(ministries of education\); connectivity and platform governance \(ICT or digital-transformation ministries\); economic competitiveness and workforce readiness \(innovation and labour portfolios\); and cyber-resilience, child protection and democratic integrity \(public-safety and justice frameworks\). Children experience the combined effects of these decisions every time they go online: the institutional boundaries between ministries are invisible to them. DL and AI literacy are a holistic government challenge with schools at its centre, and we focus on how ministries of education work alongside ICT, innovation, and public-safety actors and influence the safety and agency of young people in digital environments.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
