1125 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 660 0 R
/T (Taken together, these four functions are how schools pursue the three purposes of education set out in the Introduction, of qualification, socialisation, subjectification, and most serve more than one. The distributive function makes qualification more equitable. The pedagogical function protects the time and guidance that qualification requires, and the room for curiosity and trial and error on which subjectification depends. The relational function sustains the motivation and confidence on which both qualification and subjectification rest. The civic function carries socialisation and, by giving young people a space to test their judgement against that of others, also serves subjectification. These functions are interconnected, and AI may affect each differently. While AI can improve access to knowledge and personalise learning, it may also reduce the cognitive effort, human relationships, and shared experiences that support children's cognitive, social, and emotional development. Evaluating AI in education therefore requires looking beyond academic performance to consider its broader developmental effects. This is why we argue that schools should remain the institutional anchor of DL and AI literacy in child-centred education systems.)
/E (Taken together, these four functions are how schools pursue the three purposes of education set out in the Introduction, of qualification, socialisation, subjectification, and most serve more than one. The distributive function makes qualification more equitable. The pedagogical function protects the time and guidance that qualification requires, and the room for curiosity and trial and error on which subjectification depends. The relational function sustains the motivation and confidence on which both qualification and subjectification rest. The civic function carries socialisation and, by giving young people a space to test their judgement against that of others, also serves subjectification. These functions are interconnected, and AI may affect each differently. While AI can improve access to knowledge and personalise learning, it may also reduce the cognitive effort, human relationships, and shared experiences that support children's cognitive, social, and emotional development. Evaluating AI in education therefore requires looking beyond academic performance to consider its broader developmental effects. This is why we argue that schools should remain the institutional anchor of DL and AI literacy in child-centred education systems.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
