/P 662 0 R
/E (1.4.2. The Dual Impact of GenAI on Learning)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1144 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 662 0 R
/E (The integration of GenAI into education presents a paradox: it can improve the work students produce while undermining what they actually learn, depending on its design, implementation, and pedagogical context. The OECD \(2026a\) warns that unguided GenAI use may lead students to rely on AI for planning, monitoring and evaluating their work, rather than developing these skills themselves. In a randomised experiment with 117 university students, ChatGPT improved essay scores but did not improve knowledge acquisition, transfer of learning or intrinsic motivation \(Fan et al., 2025\). Understanding this duality is essential for designing AI-literacy curricula that maximise benefits while mitigating harms.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1145 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 662 0 R
/E (A recent expert consensus study involving 30 learning scientists and educational researchers further clarifies this duality by examining how GenAI may support or undermine specific cognitive processes involved in learning \(Kendeou et al., 2026\). The experts identified potential benefits for processes associated with consolidating knowledge \(eg., feedback, practice and self-testing\), but raised concerns about its effects on knowledge acquisition, memory retrieval, critical thinking and metacognition. These findings suggest that the impact of GenAI depends not only on how it is implemented, but also on which cognitive processes it supports or replaces. Even when GenAI improves the work students produce, it may reduce opportunities to develop the knowledge and skills needed to work independently. Critically, the study identifies elementary school students as the most vulnerable to disruptions in learning, and highlights the limited evidence on how AI affects specific learning processes.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1146 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 662 0 R
/E (Research on GenAI in education is growing, with emerging findings on its potential to support creativity, feedback and learning, though much of the evidence remains preliminary, particularly for children. Questions remain about how AI affects the cognitive processes involved in learning, and understanding both its potential benefits and risks requires examining not only what students produce with AI, but also how they learn through its use.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1147 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 662 0 R
/E (Creativity offers an example of both potential benefits and limitations. A systematic scoping review  of 22 primary studies, mostly with school-aged children, found reported benefits for divergent thinking, narrative creativity and creative self-efficacy, but the studies were small, short-term and adult-facilitated, none was longitudinal, and their designs do not allow conclusions about efficacy \(Niu et al., 2026\). In a series of co-design workshops, children aged 7 to 13 needed adults or peers to understand what the tools could do, and tended to abandon their own ideas when the outputs did not match them \(Newman et al., 2024\). Evidence of benefits for children's creative development therefore remains limited. For adults, evidence points to a further trade-off: in an online experiment with 293 writers, access to GPT-4 story ideas made short stories more creative and better written, but also more similar to one another \(Doshi & Hauser, 2024\). Whether this holds for children, whose creative and executive skills are still developing, remains to be established. )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1148 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 662 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1149 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 662 0 R
/E (AI-generated feedback may also support learning, but evidence of an advantage over human feedback is lacking. A meta-analysis of 41 studies \(4,813 students\), most of them on language and writing in higher education, found no statistically significant difference in task performance based on whether they received AI-generated or human feedback, with substantial heterogeneity, and students did not perceive AI feedback more favourably \(Kaliisa et al., 2026\).)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
