/P 666 0 R
/E (DL assessments must evolve for the AI era \(Sparks et al., 2024\). While traditional competencies such as evaluating sources and recognising misinformation remain essential, GenAI introduces additional skills to capture such as refining prompts, verifying AI-generated information, and reasoning about bias and privacy. In the 2018 PISA, fewer than one in ten 15-year-olds across OECD countries were able to distinguish between fact and opinion on the basis of implicit cues about the content or source of information \(OECD, 2021\). As GenAI introduces new ways of generating and manipulating information, assessments need to capture both established MIL competencies and emerging AI-specific skills. Without appropriate measures, policymakers and educators will struggle to identify curricular deficits, target resources, and evaluate programme effectiveness, , a gap Canadian interviewees described as a persistent limitation \(Box 3\))
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
