1245 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 670 0 R
/E (Qatar has effectively eliminated the physical digital divide: fibre networks cover 100% of the population and 5G 99% \(Qatar News Agency, 2026\), every government school is connected to high-speed fibre, and the Ministry of Education and Higher Education supplies devices to state-school students. Its assessment concludes that these inputs are necessary but not sufficient for healthy outcomes: as access became near-universal, the binding constraint shifted to wellbeing, as students report around seven hours of daily internet use, levels comparable to those in other high-income countries but now reached with near-universal access \(Chemnad et al., 2025; see Section 4.2\). )
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1246 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 670 0 R
/E (France shows a different gap. National fibre coverage is high, but many schools face an in-building connectivity bottleneck: internal Wi-Fi and cabling cannot reliably support full-class connectivity, especially in older buildings. Primary schools lag behind, with ageing desktops and limited technical support, and rural municipalities struggle to maintain equipment. )
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
