/E ( suggest combining digital-citizenship content with social and emotional learning or bullying-prevention programmes for harder-to-change behaviours. Holistic frameworks such as  DigComp 3.0 \(Cosgrove & Cachia, 2025\) offer structured guidance for curriculum development. By contrast, commentators note that programmes without a clear competency framework risk focusing only on technical skills without addressing critical thinking, ethics, or civic engagement \(Tinmaz et al., 2023\). They also risk teaching how to use specific tools rather than the concepts behind them, which leaves learners struggling to transfer their skills  when the tools change. As UNESCO \(2023b\) cautions, technology in education should not become an end goal, but should be used to prioritise pedagogical goals and learner agency.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [4]
