1270 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 672 0 R
/E (Effective DL education is not limited to technical skills; it must also address ethical, social, and emotional competencies. Inclusive design matters since AI-based tools can widen digital-literacy gaps that are already uneven, including between gender  \(OECD, 2023b\). In Canada, where Indigenous youth, newcomers and youth in rural and remote communities face digital-literacy gaps, Buchan et al. \(2024\) developed a programme and assessment tool covering digital fluency, privacy and safety, ethics and empathy, and consumer awareness. Designed for culturally diverse and remote communities, this initiative illustrates efforts to make digital-literacy education more inclusive, although its effectiveness still needs to be evaluated.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1271 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 672 0 R
/E (Inclusion is social, economic, cultural and linguistic, and its cultural and linguistic dimensions are becoming more salient as AI enters the classroom. Discussions with education ministry officials in Egypt and Qatar highlight this as well. Existing and largely Western AI models often lack the linguistic, cultural or religious nuance required in many non-Western contexts. Content that is merely translated, rather than genuinely localised, can therefore misrepresent local values, examples and norms \(Box 8\).)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1272 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 672 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
