1282 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 673 0 R
/E (Therefore, truly holistic and inclusive content design should treat culturally and linguistically responsive resources as a first-order requirement. In practice, this means investing in national or openly licensed content. This material has to be adapted with local educators and communities involved in its design and review, and with bias-checking and re-contextualisation built in, to ensure that digital literacy is taught through materials children recognise as their own. Three levers can make this possible at scale: open licences, so that resources can be adapted rather than rebuilt; national or regional platforms that aggregate adapted content across languages; and regional pooling where several countries share a language \(See Table 3.3 on Arabic-language resources and standards identified in Egypt and Qatar\).)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1283 0 obj
<<
