1434 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 680 0 R
/E (Across all four countries, the effectiveness of these organisations is constrained by a common structural vulnerability: project-based funding cycles that prevent long-term planning, workforce stability, and rigorous impact evaluation. The organisations most trusted by children and families are often the least equipped to demonstrate their value in the metrics that governments, funders, and implementing partners require. Strengthening their capacity, coordination, and evaluation is therefore not a supplementary investment but a precondition for making public systems more humane, inclusive, and resilient. Funding for these organisations should therefore be multi-year and flexible, whatever its source, and should include their capacity to evaluate impact and to co-design interventions with youth, parents, and educators. Alliances such as iRAISE can act as multistakeholder convening platforms that connect ministries and NGOs, share tools, and generate comparative evidence on what works for children in AI-mediated environments.)
/Pg 49 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
