1440 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 681 0 R
/E (The first answer is time: the investments that matter most take decades to pay off. Education is among the public investments with the highest and best-documented returns, about 9% per additional year of schooling on average across 139 countries \(Psacharopoulos & Patrinos, 2018\). However, these returns accrue over decades, whereas electoral mandates typically last four to five years, a mismatch political scientists identify as a structural obstacle to long-term policy investment \(Jacobs, 2011\).)
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1441 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 681 0 R
/E (Research on public spending in general, not only on education, shows the same pattern around elections. Across 67 democracies, the growth of public investment peaks around 28 months before an election and slows as the election approaches \(Gupta et al., 2016\). A study of 19 established OECD democracies found that elections tend to shift public spending towards expenses with immediate benefits for voters, such as wages, transfers and subsidies, and away from longer-term investments such as infrastructure \(Katsimi & Sarantides, 2012\). This constraint may be tighter for EU countries, as governments now stay in office for shorter periods and snap elections have become more frequent \(Lehne, 2026\), as illustrated by early elections in the Netherlands \(2023, 2025\), France \(2024\), Portugal \(2024, 2025\) and Germany \(2025\). Shorter and less predictable political horizons are likely to make it even harder to sustain investments whose returns take decades to materialise.)
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1442 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 681 0 R
/T (Investing in teachers, their training, and in dedicated time for digital and AI literacy.)
/E (Investing in teachers, their training, and in dedicated time for digital and AI literacy.)
/Pg 50 0 R
