1683 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 693 0 R
/E (The report has three main limitations: evidence and causality, participants, and generalisability of findings. First, for evidence and causality, much of the quantitative evidence is correlational and draws from cross-sectional studies and meta-analyses of associations. Those studies show that digital literacy and outcomes tend to move together, such as for achievement, online safety, and wellbeing, but do not establish causation. Reverse causation and confounding factors \(e.g., socioeconomic status, school quality, home environment\) are plausible and rarely fully controlled. The consistency and direction of the evidence are nonetheless sufficient to guide investment, and this is precisely why Chapter IV calls for the longitudinal and quasi-experimental research needed to test these relationships directly. Second, children, adolescents and parents were not consulted directly, so their perspectives entered only through the officials, NGOs and experts we consulted and through the survey literature. General Comment No. 25 asks States to involve children and give due weight to their views when developing policies on the digital environment \(UN Committee on the Rights of the Child, 2021\), and future editions should include them. Third, the four countries cover different regions and governance models but do not allow findings to be generalised as the report does not include low-income countries, and no Asian or South American education system, so findings may transfer less readily to systems with very different resources or institutions.)
/Pg 62 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1684 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 693 0 R
/T (Limitations)
/E (Limitations)
/Pg 62 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
