>>
endobj
1686 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 693 0 R
/T (CONCLUSION: HUMAN-CENTRED AI LITERACY IN SCHOOLS)
/E (CONCLUSION: HUMAN-CENTRED AI LITERACY IN SCHOOLS)
/Pg 62 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1687 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 693 0 R
/E (By adopting a mixed-methods approach, this report integrates insights from scientific research, policy documents, interviews and consultations with government officials, NGOs and experts, and in-depth case studies to tackle how DL and AI literacy can be holistically approached. Across the report, three converging insights emerge.)
/Pg 62 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1688 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 693 0 R
/E (First, DL and AI literacy need to be considered as core education infrastructure. If countries treat them as peripheral or purely technical, they risk leaving a generation of young people dependent on opaque systems they cannot question or fully understand, at the expense of cognitive autonomy, mental health, and democratic participation.)
/Pg 62 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1689 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 693 0 R
/E (Second, infrastructure and content alone are not sufficient. The four case studies \(France, Canada, Egypt, and Qatar\) suggest that outcomes depend as much on human factors as on policy design. These factors encompass teacher confidence, parental mediation, youth trust, and the ability of NGOs and communities to translate abstract frameworks into safe, meaningful practice.)
/Pg 62 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1690 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 693 0 R
/E (Third, GenAI makes the relational dimension of teaching even more important. Students can now find information and have it explained to them at any time, but they still need adults who help them focus, persevere and judge what they are told. Because AI can rapidly generate plausible answers and emotional simulations, the distinctive value of teachers, caregivers, and mentors lies in modelling critical thinking, ethical judgement, and emotionally attuned support, so that young people become subjects who reason for themselves and answer for their actions. Effective DL programmes must therefore be designed to teach children how to use digital tools )
/Pg 62 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
