2173 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 708 0 R
/E (This model enables strong normative coherence, as every student is assessed against the same competencies, but it creates deep territorial inequalities in the actual conditions for digital learning, especially in primary schools and rural areas. This tension is intensifying due to a demographic slump \(see A.1\) that creates mechanical pressures to close classes in rural areas. Digital continuity tools and hybrid learning are therefore becoming strategically vital for maintaining educational equity in depopulating territories.)
/Pg 77 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
2174 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 708 0 R
/T (A.4. Remaining challenges)
/E (A.4. Remaining challenges)
/Pg 77 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
2175 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 708 0 R
/E (Interviews with French Ministry of Education delegates highlight four main challenges.)
/Pg 77 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
2176 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 708 0 R
/E (First, territorial inequality in primary schools persists: rural municipalities struggle to maintain equipment, leading to obsolete or non-functional devices and widening gaps in early digital exposure. For a primary-school student in rural France, this inequality is not abstract; it means that the digital tools available to a peer in Lyon or Paris may simply not exist in her classroom, narrowing her early opportunities to develop the critical and creative skills that the CRCN framework promises to all children equally.)
/Pg 77 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
